msgid "Dev Assistant"
msgstr ""

msgid "General overview"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Quarkus Dev Assistant is an AI-powered helper built directly into "
"Quarkus Dev Mode. Its goal is to make you more productive by helping you "
"troubleshoot and assisting with development tasks."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The assistant runs **only** within dev mode and never affects your "
"production application."
msgstr ""

msgid "What Can It Do?"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Dev Assistant can help with:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Troubleshooting exceptions and stack traces"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Generating example client code (e.g., `javascript` client for REST "
"endpoints)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Generating javadoc for your code"
msgstr ""

msgid "Generating test code for your application"
msgstr ""

msgid "Generating test data for your application"
msgstr ""

msgid "Explain existing code in you application"
msgstr ""

msgid "Complete `//TODO` sections in your code"
msgstr ""

msgid "... and more to come !"
msgstr ""

msgid "How to Add the Dev Assistant"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To enable the Dev Assistant in your Quarkus project, add the `quarkus-"
"chappie` extension:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Replace `${chappie-extension-version}` with the latest available version. "
"You can find the latest version on https://quarkus.io/extensions/io."
"quarkiverse.chappie/quarkus-chappie/[extensions.quarkus.io]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Once added, open the Dev UI to start using the assistant."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The assistant interfaces are defined in Quarkus \"core\", but their "
"implementation are located in Quarkiverse (Chappie). You can, if needed, "
"provide an alternative implementation of the assistant."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuration and Providers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, the assistant is disabled until configured. You can choose from "
"supported AI providers:"
msgstr ""

msgid "OpenAI (e.g., ChatGPT, Openshift AI, Podman AI Lab, ...)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Ollama (local models like `llama3`)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Configuration can be done directly in Dev UI via the assistant settings pane."
" You'll need:"
msgstr ""

msgid "An API key (for OpenAI)"
msgstr ""

msgid "A local running model (for Ollama or Podman AI)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Settings are stored in `~/.quarkus/chappie/chappie-assistant.properties`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Dev Assistant integrates seamlessly into various parts of the Dev UI. "
"Wherever assistant features are available, they are highlighted using a "
"distinctive *pink theme*, making it easy to spot AI-powered interactions."
msgstr ""

msgid "Currently, assistant functions are available in:"
msgstr ""

msgid "The *Workspace* view"
msgstr ""

msgid "Extension pages that support AI-driven help"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Look for pink-highlighted buttons or badges - these indicate actions powered "
"by the Dev Assistant."
msgstr ""

msgid "Exception help"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When an error or exception occurs, the error page includes a `Get help with "
"this` button."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Clicking it takes you directly to the Dev UI's Assistant panel, pre-filled "
"with relevant error context. This allows the assistant to help you:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Understand what the exception means"
msgstr ""

msgid "Suggest likely causes"
msgstr ""

msgid "Recommend how to fix the issue"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This provides a fast and focused troubleshooting workflow without needing to "
"copy and paste logs manually."
msgstr ""

msgid "Exception Help in the Log"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In addition to error page support, the Dev Assistant can also help with "
"exceptions via the quarkus log."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When an exception is detected, you can press the `h` key in the quarkus log "
"to open the *shortcut menu*. Under the `Assistant` heading, you'll see a new "
"option like:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Press `a` to send the exception to the assistant for analysis."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is especially useful if the exception occurred outside a visible page "
"or during startup. The assistant will analyze the error and suggest "
"potential causes or fixes — without needing to navigate elsewhere."
msgstr ""

msgid "Assistant log"
msgstr ""

msgid "You can access the log of the assistant in the Dev UI Footer:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Guide for Extension Developers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus extensions can enhance the developer experience by contributing to "
"the Dev Assistant. This section explains how to integrate your extension "
"with the Dev Assistant, covering:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Workspace-based assistant actions"
msgstr ""

msgid "Assistant-specific pages"
msgstr ""

msgid "Backend integration via JSON-RPC"
msgstr ""

msgid "UI features for assistant-aware components"
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus log participation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An extension can define Assistant features without depending on Chappie "
"directly. The interfaces and associated structures are located in the "
"`assistant` extension."
msgstr ""

msgid "Workspace participation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can add an assistant function to a workspace item using the same "
"approach as regular xref:dev-ui.adoc#workspace[workspace actions], with one "
"key difference: instead of using `.function(...)`, you use `."
"assistantFunction(...)`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use `assistantFunction` to receive the `Assistant` instance and xref:dev-ui."
"adoc#input[input] parameters."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Provide an optional *system message* to guide the assistant's behavior."
msgstr ""

msgid "Provide a *user message* as the primary prompt."
msgstr ""

msgid "Provide a class defining your expected response structure."
msgstr ""

msgid "Assistant pages"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To add a standalone assistant-powered page to the Dev UI, use the xref:dev-"
"ui.adoc#card[`CardPageBuildItem`] and `Page.assistantPageBuilder()`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the assistant page builder."
msgstr ""

msgid "Communicating to the backend"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can invoke the assistant from backend code using xref:dev-ui."
"adoc#communicating-to-the-backend[standard Dev UI JSON-RPC] patterns — both "
"against the *runtime* and *deployment* classpaths."
msgstr ""

msgid "JsonRPC against the Runtime classpath"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To use the assistant in a xref:dev-ui.adoc#jsonrpc-against-the-runtime-"
"classpath[`JsonRpcService`] on the runtime classpath, you need to make sure "
"the `Assistant` interface is available:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Then you can inject the `Assistant` like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Inject the assistant as optional, it will only be present if a provider is "
"added and configured."
msgstr ""

msgid "You can now use this assistant in any JsonRPC method, example:"
msgstr ""

msgid "JsonRPC against the Deployment classpath"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In xref:dev-ui.adoc#jsonrpc-against-the-deployment-classpath[deployment-"
"time] code, you need to add the assistant-deployment-spi:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Then use the `BuildTimeActionBuildItem` and register assistant actions via `."
"addAssistantAction(...)`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use `assistantFunction` instead of `function` to access the assistant "
"context."
msgstr ""

msgid "Assistant State in the UI"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To conditionally render assistant UI in your Web Component, you can use the "
"xref:dev-ui.adoc#assistant-state[assistant state] to\n"
"check the state of the assistant. The state can be:"
msgstr ""

msgid "notAvailable: No assistant implementation (like Chappie) is present."
msgstr ""

msgid "available: Assistant is available but not yet configured."
msgstr ""

msgid "ready: Assistant is configured and ready to use."
msgstr ""

msgid "To use this state in your page:"
msgstr ""

msgid "import observeState from the LitState library."
msgstr ""

msgid "import the state you are interested in, in this case assistant state."
msgstr ""

msgid "Wrap the LitElement in `observerState`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Now you can access the assistant state anywhere in your page, and when that "
"state changes, it will act exactly the same as a local state - re-render the "
"relevant parts of the page:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Assistant themes and UI Components"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To maintain visual consistency with other Dev Assistant features, use the "
"following components and styles:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`<qui-assistant-button>`: A pre-styled pink button with a robot icon."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`<qui-assistant-warning>`: A standard warning message with the text: "
"`\"Quarkus assistant can make mistakes. Check responses.\"`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`<qui-assistant-chat>`: A button that deep-link to the chat screen with the "
"current chat.`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also use the CSS var `--quarkus-assistant` anywhere you want to "
"apply the assistant's theme color."
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus Log Participation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Extensions can contribute entries to the interactive *Quarkus log menu* by "
"integrating with the Dev Assistant.\n"
"This allows developers to invoke assistant-powered actions directly from the "
"quarkus log using keyboard shortcuts."
msgstr ""

